systemd 262 for Fil-C. Rebased from upstream Fil-C's systemd 256.4 patch
(ports/patch/systemd-256.4.patch), plus what 260 needs, rebased onto 262:

- meson.build: link with --version-script= (upstream), and pass the
  src/include/override headers with -I: the Fil-C driver puts
  -isystem <glibc>/include ahead of the build's flags, which would hide
  them behind glibc's headers.
- signal-util.c: skip the signals the runtime reserves when resetting
  handlers (upstream).
- sigbus.c: Fil-C reserves SIGBUS, so there is no handler to install; a
  SIGBUS on a truncated mmap'd journal kills the reader instead.
- process-util.c: no pidfd_spawn(); the Fil-C glibc's asserts on it. Plain
  posix_spawn() works.
- src/libc: Fil-C's syscall() stops the program on a number it does not
  support. 262 generates the raw wrappers with DEFINE_SYSCALL_SHIM; the
  ones where systemd has fallbacks (bpf, ioprio, kcmp, open_tree_attr,
  pivot_root, quotactl_fd, setxattrat, removexattrat, rt_tgsigqueueinfo)
  use DEFINE_FILC_ENOSYS_SYSCALL_SHIM, which under Fil-C reports ENOSYS
  when libc lacks the symbol instead of calling syscall().
- capability-util.c, reboot-util.c, raw-clone.c: the same for the raw
  capget/capset, reboot and clone calls, which PID 1 makes. capget and
  capset go through glibc's wrappers; reboot through glibc's reboot()
  (ENOSYS when given a RESTART2 argument; callers fall back); raw_clone
  forks and unshare()s the child into its new namespaces, and refuses
  CLONE_NEWPID, which cannot be had that way.
- random-util.c, osc-context.c, build-path.c: getauxval() returns
  addresses as plain integers under Fil-C, which cannot be dereferenced.
  Skip the AT_RANDOM bytes (only mixed into a fallback seed and an ID that
  the pid and pidfd ID keep unique) and report no runpath instead of
  walking AT_PHDR.
- process-util.c: clone_with_nested_stack() returns ENOSYS; glibc's
  clone() stops a Fil-C program. asynchronous_close() then closes in
  place.
- bus-error.c/.h: look the bus error maps up in a static list instead of
  an ELF section (upstream's approach, simplified).
- sd-dlopen.h (262): SD_ELF_NOTE_DLOPEN_ANCHORED's anchor symbol is
  defined in assembly, which a Fil-C C reference cannot reach; use the
  plain note, as on clang before 18. That note is inline assembly inside
  each dlopen_*() function, which stops a Fil-C program when reached, so
  under Fil-C it is a static string instead; ports/restore-dlopen-notes.py
  turns those back into notes.
- options.h, verbs.h, options.c, verbs.c, src/shared/meson.build: 262
  collects its option and verb tables in ELF sections and walks them from
  __start_ to __stop_, which Fil-C cannot do (docs/filc-findings.md).
  Under Fil-C each table registers itself from a constructor into an array
  in options.c or verbs.c, one per module: libsystemd-shared is module 1
  and the program is module 0. Verbs are file-scope stanzas, so the Fil-C
  _VERB_DATA() needs no source change. Options are block-scope statics in
  each parse_argv(), so patches/systemd-filc-option-tables.py, which the
  port runs in postPatch, moves each function's stanzas into a file-scope
  array registered just before it and leaves "case 0x100 + N" labels.
- verbs.h, verbs.c: a pointer cast to uintptr_t loses its Fil-C
  capability, so COMMAND() keeps its CommandDescription in a Fil-C-only
  Verb.command field instead of in data.
- efi-api.c: built without EFI (the port's withEfi = false),
  efi_has_tpm2() returned -EOPNOTSUPP from a bool function, which reads
  as true, so creds, cryptsetup and kmod-setup assumed a firmware TPM2.
  It returns false instead. This is an upstream bug, not a Fil-C one.
- process-util.c: prctl_safe() passes its arguments as unsigned long,
  which drops a pointer's capability, so the three calls that hand prctl()
  a buffer call it directly.

